Scenario
The PSHS school canteen is small and often gets crowded during lunch break. Students line up to buy food, but the process is slow because:

- Some students take too long to decide what to order.
- The cashier has to manually calculate totals and give change.
- There is no system to track which food items are running out.

Your group’s task is to decompose this problem into smaller, manageable parts that could be solved with computational thinking (CT) Skills.

Step 1: Identify the Big Problem
Main Problem: ____The problem is mostly due to the big amount of time taken just to wait and take the orders of the students, as well as the lack of resources for accessing a faster system to take orders, calculate totals and changes, and to track food storage, resulting to the use of manual listing. It is also time consuming and tiring.

Step 2: Identify three to four Sub-Problems
Please list possible sub-problems:

1) The lack of cashiers and canteen staff or workers to take orders and speed up the process.
2) Canteen staff or workers feeling stressed and rushed, which slows down work efficiency.
3) It is possible that there are no pre - packed meals or ready to go meals which slows down the process of ordering, paying, and taking the food.
4) All students may have the same recess break period which makes the canteen even more full and packed.

